[Music]  in 2019 oil giant bp began a huge  project that would pay dozens of  communities across mexico to protect  their trees and reforest using carbon  offsets wall street's favorite climate  solution each offset would represent a  ton of carbon absorbed thanks to the  promise of payment in these low-income  communities however when bp made its  first payment last year it gave  villagers just a fraction of market  rates  [Music]  the mountain community of coyotetila in  eastern mexico used to be cradled by  rich pine forests but over the years  villagers had to chip away at the trees  for logging and farm work in a place  where on the rare occasions that can  find work the average pay is just 6.50  per day  [Music]  dozens of villagers across mexico  thought they found a way to protect  their forests and earn crucial income  when they were approached by officials  from bp's project  integrated  became one of the first communities to  be paid by the program but at just over  forty dollars per person it was a  fraction of what they've been hoping for  and a climate bargain for bp which could  sell the offsets for three or four times  that amount on the market  is  [Music]  ep says the program was helping develop  rural areas but the low pay was a blow  for a community where most young men  have to leave their home for work  [Music]  the low pay doesn't just harm the  villages it also damages the chances of  the program's environmental success  which depend on the forest being  preserved for decades  for example  a few weeks after the people of kurta  complained about the low prices they  were told that bp would be increasing  their pay  the program's administrators confirmed  to bloomberg that the villagers would be  getting a raise but wouldn't provide  details  the increase didn't stop mexico's  president weighing in on the story  [Music]  yes  [Music]  for now the community is hoping for a  better second payment while struggling  with soaring inflation  at present nearly all of this young men  spend the year travelling for labour  they hardly see their kids grow up and  poor pay robs them with the chance to  spend more time at home with their  families

